      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle, we start by understanding the properties of the ellipse. The lengths of the major and minor axes are 10 and 8, respectively. The semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) are given by:\n\\[ a = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{8}{2} = 4 \\]\n\nThe distance \\(c\\) from the center to each focus of the ellipse is given by:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\nThe foci of the ellipse are located at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\). The smallest circle centered at a focus that is tangent to the ellipse will have a radius equal to the distance from the focus to the point on the ellipse that is closest to the focus. This point is located at the endpoint of the minor axis, which is \\((0, 4)\\).\n\nWe need to find the distance from the focus \\((3, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\). Using the distance formula, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
